    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c1cd8597_c9cf29d6",
        "filename": "keymint/aidl/android/hardware/keymint/IKeyMintOperation.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 247,
      "author": {
        "id": 1092085
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-09-04T05:52:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Out byte arrays are curious things. In C++ they are represented as std:;vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e*, in rust, they are represented as \u0026mug Vec\u003cu8\u003e. Both allow the caller to pass in a empty object that gets resized by the callee/libbinder to fit the transmitted data.\n\nThe Java API however translates this into ..., byte[] output, ... Which means that the caller has to allocate an array that is large enough for the anticipated data. (I looked at the code and it must be exactly the anticipated size.)\n\nOne way to address this would be to make byte[] the return type. In this case the array can be allocated by the callee.\nAnother would be to add an indirection, e.g., parcelable Bytes { byte[] data } as type for output. The caller would then allocate an empty Bytes object which can be modified with a new array.\nYet another would be to assume that KeyMint has no java clients. In that case we should switch off java code generation in the Android.bp file.\n\nThe same is true for outParams and for the same parameters in `update()`.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 247,
        "startChar": 46,
        "endLine": 247,
        "endChar": 63
      },
      "revId": "87210349393148aaf6b4075070fe54d4e7bbee51",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
