      "writtenOn": "2020-09-08T17:27:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Note that EXPECT_* macros do not perform control flow. Which means that the content of result.token will be checked even if the call failed, i.e., result.error !\u003d OK.\n\nWe use EXPECT in test helper functions, because ASSERT_* macros return void, so they cannot convey failures to the caller. But this also means that control flow needs to be done explicitly. This here, however, is an actual test function, so you should use ASSERT_* macros throughout, in order to abort on the first error.\n\nWith std::variant this could look like this:\n\n if (auto token_ptr \u003d result1.get_if\u003cVerificationToken\u003e()) {\n     ASSERT_EQ(1U, token_ptr-\u003echallenge);\n     ASSERT_EQ(SecLevel(), token_ptr-\u003esecurityLevel);\n     ASSERT_GT(token_ptr-\u003etimestamp.milliSeconds, 0U);\n } else if (auto error_ptr \u003d result1.get_if\u003cErrorCode\u003e()) {\n     // if we reach this part *error_ptr must not be OK,\n     // so if we fail here we must fail on the second ASSERT.\n     ASSERT_NE(ErrorCode::OK, *error_ptr);\n     ASSERT_EQ(ErrorCode::OK, *error_ptr);\n } else {\n     // This can never happen.\n     FAIL;\n }",
      "revId": "87210349393148aaf6b4075070fe54d4e7bbee51",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
